s  always advocated and continues to  advocate for the closest possible future  relationship between Britain and Ireland  and Britain in the EU we don't want to  see any unnecessary checks between  Britain and and Ireland but if Britain  is determined to leave the customs union  in single market well then some checks  are unavoidable last week I spoke to  David Davis the former brexit secretary  who said he was very hopeful of getting  a deal as well but he said that the  situation in Ireland was an inconvenient  exaggeration and that things weren't as  difficult to sort out as they've been  painted out effectively is he right well  I mean well I hope he's right that we  can resolve these things in the next two  weeks before the next EU leaders meeting  but I'm afraid it is complex you know  I'm afraid anybody anybody who who  understands the last 20 years in  Northern Ireland since the Good Friday  Agreement  the fragility of politics and community  relations in Northern Ireland the fact  that we haven't had devolved government  in Northern Ireland for nearly 22 months  now it is a very difficult political  environment relationships and the island  of Ireland historically are complex and  so we need to make sure that nothing  happens as an unintended consequence of  Briggs's that damages or undermines  relationships in the island of Ireland  that so many people have worked so hard  to protect and I think Teresa made to  her credit understands that and will  follow through on the commitments that  she's made to Ireland and a quick  thought from you because of course in  the UK there has been some consternation  perhaps about some of the actions or  Donald tusks the president of the EU  Council now he effectively posted an  Instagram picture didn't he where he  poked fun at Teresa May with a with a  picture of her looking at some cakes  talking about cherry picking this was at  a time when she was effectively  humiliated in Salzburg did you look at  that and think he